# series — the linux-patches/ subset applied to the 7.2 beta kernel.
# Buildroot's apply-patches.sh honors this file: only patches listed here
# are applied, in order. Entries are symlinks to the shared
# board/mister/de10nano/linux-patches/ files (single source of truth).
#
# Excluded vs the 6.18 set: EXACTLY ONE, and it is the only kind of exclusion
# that can ever be safe — 0047-btusb-mercusys-ma530-2c4e-0115.patch, which is
# not fork code at all but a BACKPORT of mainline ce21a5cf3d1f, whose first
# release is 7.2. This kernel IS 7.2, so it already has those two lines in
# drivers/bluetooth/btusb.c. Measured, not assumed: at -F0 against pristine
# v7.2 the hunk does not apply twice, it FAILS ("Hunk #1 FAILED at 786"), so
# listing it here would break `make rt` at patch time. The 6.18 image needs it
# because 6.18.y never got the commit (no Cc: stable) — see the patch's own
# Upstream: block. When the pin for the STOCK kernel finally leaves 6.18.y,
# that patch is deleted from linux-patches/ outright and this exclusion goes
# with it.
# The beta series therefore carries 36 of the 37 shared patches plus the 4
# beta-local ones below — still 40 entries — so within the carried set the 7.x
# and 6.18 kernels diverge only where an upstream API forced a re-anchor.
# 0038-0042 were the last gap: they landed in the shared dir on 2026-07-24,
# went unlisted here for three weeks, and were closed on 2026-08-17 once
# measured rather than assumed. They needed NO re-anchor — plain symlinks,
# applied on 7.2 final at -F0 with offsets only (0038 1, 0039 1, 0040 1,
# 0041 2, 0042 5 hunks at an offset, zero fuzz), and `make rt` is green on the
# whole 40 from a clean tree. See docs/rt-beta-kernel.md §6.
# Keep it that way. The rule is unchanged and the 0047 exclusion above does not
# soften it: if a patch will not apply to a newer 7.x release, RE-ANCHOR it here
# rather than removing it from this list. "It does not apply" is never by itself
# a reason to drop — 0015 and 0037 were both dropped on that reasoning once and
# both were wrong. The single admissible reason to omit a shared patch is the
# one 0047 has: the change is already IN the 7.x tree because it came from
# mainline and the beta pin is at or past its first release. That is checkable
# in one command (grep the pristine tarball for the added lines) and must be
# checked, not inferred from a subject line.
#
# The list now runs 0037 -> 0038 with no gap. 0043, 0044, 0045 and 0046
# are BETA-LOCAL — real files
# in THIS directory, deliberately absent from the shared linux-patches/ set so
# the stock 6.18 build (which globs that whole dir via BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_PATCH)
# never sees them. Apply-order facts, precisely: 0043 anchors on 0004's
# output; 0044 anchors on 0043's output; 0045 touches only drivers/uio and
# include files no other patch in this series touches, so it applies
# independently at any position; 0046 touches the same DTS as 0043/0044 but
# anchors on the memory@0 node, which sits ABOVE everything those two insert
# and which no patch in this series modifies — so its hunk lands at the same
# line whether or not they have been applied, and its position is free too.
# All four apply fine over the hole. Together they are the RT line's reason to
# exist right now (ADR-002-interim and ADR-003, Main_MiSTer rewrite plan):
#
#   0043  UIO doorbell nodes on GIC SPI 48..55 (f2h_irq8..15). Interrupt-only
#         nodes; a blocking read() on /dev/uioN replaces the cause-register
#         poll over the lightweight bridge. The pool sat at SPI 41..48 until
#         2026-08-14 — stock gateware drives BOTH low f2h lines
#         (HDMI_TX_VS and video_sync, sys_top.v:573), so the old split had a
#         UIO node exclusively claiming a live ~60 Hz line. Renumbered, not
#         renamed: the node names are userspace ABI and stayed
#         mister_doorbell1..8, which are now pool ordinals rather than line
#         numbers.
#   0044  reg-bearing UIO nodes for the 2 MB lightweight-bridge register
#         window and the 512 MiB shared DDR3 aperture — named, size-bounded
#         mappings in place of /dev/mem. Memory only, no interrupts. Must
#         sort after 0043: it anchors on the last doorbell node.
#   0045  UIO_MEM_PHYS_WC, a write-combining page attribute for UIO physical
#         maps, selected by the "mister,map-writecombine" property 0044 puts
#         on the aperture node (and deliberately not on the register-window
#         node). Touches drivers/uio/, include/linux/ and the uio-howto
#         documentation only, no DTS, so its position is free — listed last
#         because 0044 is what gives the property a consumer. Without 0045
#         that property is inert and the aperture maps strongly-ordered:
#         slower, never unsafe.
#   0046  a /reserved-memory ramoops node at 0x1FE00000+0x100000 — the last
#         MiB below the mem=511M cap — split 23x32 KiB dmesg records /
#         256 KiB console / 32 KiB pmsg. Unrelated to the UIO set: it is
#         there because hardware finding H-1 hung the board three times, once
#         with a serial console attached and capturing, and produced ZERO
#         bytes of output each time. The UART stops being serviced at the
#         instant of a hang; DRAM does not, and is readable on the next boot.
#         Needs CONFIG_PSTORE / CONFIG_PSTORE_RAM / CONFIG_PSTORE_CONSOLE /
#         CONFIG_PSTORE_PMSG
#         from ../linux-rt.fragment — which is also why it must stay
#         beta-local, since in the stock DTB the node would reserve 1 MiB of
#         every user's RAM for a subsystem that is not compiled in.
#
# ONE NUMBERING NAMESPACE, TWO DIRECTORIES. 0043-0046 being beta-local is why
# the next SHARED patch took 0047 rather than 0043: a number means one patch
# across both directories, so a reader who sees "0044" never has to ask which
# 0044. The shared dir therefore reads 0001-0042 then 0047, with the 0043-0046
# hole reserved. Applying order is unaffected — the shared dir is globbed and
# sorted, so 0047 simply applies last there, and it is not in this series at all.
#
# NOT-symlink exceptions: four of the shared-patch entries are REAL FILES
# re-anchored for 7.x (0043-0046 above are real files too, but they are
# beta-local originals, not re-anchors of anything) —
# the shared 6.18 patches are deliberately untouched (parity with stock).
# Buildroot applies patches with `patch -F0` (fuzz zero), and these four
# needed either re-anchored context or a real 7.x API/header delta. Keep
# each in lockstep with its ../linux-patches/ original (every copy carries
# a bracketed re-anchor note).
#
# 0031 WAS a fifth; it is a symlink again as of 2026-07-25. Worth reading
# before adding a sixth, because the same trick may apply: rather than
# re-anchoring the 7.x copy around the drifted context, the SHARED patch
# was moved to anchor somewhere both trees agree (it now defines
# exfat_symlink_inode_operations *before* exfat_file_inode_operations
# instead of after, because 7.x had both added a struct member and
# retabbed the alignment, leaving no common trailing context). Same
# resulting source, one file. A re-anchored copy is the fallback, not the
# first move — ask first whether the shared patch can be anchored on
# something stabler:
#   0001-fbdev-add-MiSTer_fb-driver     — one-line delta: 7.x removed
#     <linux/fbcon.h>; fbcon_update_vcs() now comes from the fbdev-core
#     header ("core/fbcon.h").
#   0015-hid-nintendo-nso-famicom       — an earlier port test called this
#     "upstreamed in 7.2"; that was WRONG (7.2 has no FAML/FAMR 0x07/0x08 —
#     its left/right nescon types are 0x09/0x0A, a different thing), so it
#     is re-included here, re-anchored (enum gained LIC_PRO; dispatch sites'
#     preceding procon branches grew a LIC_PRO conditional).
#   0030-i2c-designware-quiet-timeout   — 7.x renamed i2c_dw_init_master()
#     to i2c_dw_init(), which appears in both the trailing comment and the
#     call right after the changed line, so the after-context missed at -F0.
#   0037-hid-playstation-dualsense-mute-btn-z — 7.x added the DualSense
#     Edge back-paddle/Fn blocks at both sites this patch anchors on
#     (parse_report and dualsense_create), breaking hunks 3 and 5 at -F0.
#     Previously omitted here as "cosmetic"; that was WRONG. BTN_Z (0x135)
#     sits between BTN_WEST and BTN_TL, so declaring it shifts every higher
#     gamepad button one index up in the EV_KEY bitmap — and Main_MiSTer
#     resolves gamecontrollerdb's SDL-style bN indices off that bitmap.
#     The shipped gamecontrollerdb.txt "platform:MiSTer" PS5 rows encode
#     the BTN_Z-present layout (guide:b11), so omitting this patch silently
#     slides the whole DualSense map by one: PS/Home acts as Start, L3
#     opens the OSD. Any patch adding/removing an EV_KEY or EV_ABS
#     capability is load-bearing for those index maps — never classify one
#     as cosmetic on a symbol grep alone.
#
